Every card in this deck hurts everyone at the table — you included. That is the point, not the problem. Torbran adds 2 to every hit a red source of yours lands on an opponent or their permanents, so Earthquake for 3 costs you 3 and costs each opponent 5, and a ping for 1 is a 3-for-1 against every player at once. Many small hits beat one big one, because the +2 is per instance.
One rule decides every number below. When two effects change damage to an opponent, THAT OPPONENT chooses the order (CR 616.1) — and they will pick the smaller result: double first, then add. So a 1-damage ping with Torbran and Twinflame Tyrant deals 4 (1×2+2), not 6.

# The commander
Torbran, Thane of Red Fell :: red sources you control deal +2 to opponents and anything they control. Only RED sources — Valakut, the Molten Pinnacle is a land, and lands are colourless, so it gets nothing from Torbran.
Lightning Greaves :: haste and shroud for Torbran the turn it lands — the table knows exactly what it does.
Swiftfoot Boots :: hexproof when you need to keep targeting Torbran yourself.
Deflecting Swat :: free with your commander out, and it sends their removal back at them.

# Stacking the damage — every one of these is one-sided
All seven only boost damage to OPPONENTS. None of them makes your own Earthquake hurt you more. That is why Fiery Emancipation is not in this deck — it triples damage to you from your own sources too.
Twinflame Tyrant :: doubles damage from ANY source you control, colourless included. With Torbran, a ping of 1 becomes 4.
Solphim, Mayhem Dominus :: doubles noncombat damage — which is nearly all of this deck. Twinflame Tyrant and Solphim together turn a ping of 1 into 6 (1×2×2+2).
Fiendish Duo :: doubles damage to your opponents from ANY source, even their own. When they burn each other, it counts double too.
Mechanized Warfare :: +1 on red and artifact sources. With Torbran, a ping of 1 is 4.
Thor, Asgard's Avenger :: +1 for every other source you control, colourless ones included.
Fated Firepower :: flash in at instant speed with X fire counters, and every hit gets +X. On a big mana turn it adds more than the rest put together.
Ojer Axonil, Deepest Might :: any red noncombat hit on an opponent below Ojer's power becomes Ojer's power. With Torbran out, the opponent orders it Torbran-then-Ojer, so a ping of 1 deals 4. When Ojer dies it comes back as a land.

# Damage to every player
The core of the deck. Each of these hits you once and each opponent once plus Torbran's 2.
Earthquake :: X to every player and every creature without flying. X of 5 is 5 to you and 7 to each opponent — and it wipes their ground board.
Rolling Earthquake :: the same, and it hits fliers too.
Flame Rift :: two mana, 4 to you and 6 to each opponent.
Volcanic Fallout :: an instant that cannot be countered — 2 to you, 4 to each opponent and each of their creatures.
Slagstorm :: pick the mode. 3 to every player (5 to each opponent), or 3 to every creature as a sweeper.
Price of Progress :: 2 for every nonbasic land each player controls. Opponents run twenty-plus; this deck runs six. It is a one-card finisher against a greedy mana base.
Pyrohemia :: {R}: 1 to every creature and every player — 3 to each opponent and their creatures each activation. The best mana sink in the deck.
Sulfuric Vortex :: 2 to each player on their own upkeep (4 to opponents), and nobody gains life.
Roiling Vortex :: a point to every player each upkeep, 5 to anyone who casts a spell for free, and {R} stops opponents gaining life for a turn.
Manabarbs :: 1 damage every time a player taps a land — 3 for opponents. An opponent tapping seven lands takes 21.
Descent into Avernus :: 2, then 4, then 6 damage to every player on your successive upkeeps, and the same number of Treasures for everyone. Opponents take 2 more each time; the Treasures fuel you as much as them.
Themberchaud :: when it enters, damage equal to your Mountain count to every other creature without flying and every player. This deck runs 28 Mountains.
Ashling the Pilgrim :: {1}{R} for a counter; the third activation in a turn removes them all and deals that much to every creature and every player. Grow it across turns, then detonate.
Heartless Hidetsugu :: taps to deal every player half their life, rounded down. With Torbran and ONE doubler out, that kills every opponent at once — see the combos.
Arcbond :: choose a creature; whenever it is dealt damage this turn, it deals that much to every other creature and every player. On Brash Taunter before an Earthquake it turns one card into two sweepers.

# Engines — every creature, spell or card drawn hits them
Impact Tremors :: every creature you play deals 1 to each opponent — 3 with Torbran.
Purphoros, God of the Forge :: 2 to each opponent per creature, so 4. With Impact Tremors too, one creature is 7 to every opponent.
Terror of the Peaks :: each creature that enters deals its power to any target.
Warstorm Surge :: the same trigger, on an enchantment that is harder to remove.
Goblin Chainwhirler :: on entry, 3 to each opponent and to EVERY creature and planeswalker they control. It wipes most boards of small creatures.
Guttersnipe :: every instant and sorcery you cast is 4 to each opponent. This deck casts a lot of sorceries.
Razorkin Needlehead :: 3 to an opponent for every card they draw, their own draw step included.
Goblin Bombardment :: sacrifice a creature to deal 3 anywhere. It turns a chump blocker into burn and a combo into a kill.

# The combos — this is why the deck is Bracket 3.5
Kiki-Jiki, Mirror Breaker :: copy Zealous Conscripts; the copy untaps Kiki-Jiki; repeat. Unlimited hasty 3/3s.
Zealous Conscripts :: the other half, and a threaten effect on its own.
Dualcaster Mage :: cast Twinflame or Heat Shimmer on any creature you control, then flash Dualcaster in with it still on the stack. Dualcaster copies the spell onto ITSELF, the hasty token copies it again, and so on. Unlimited hasty 2/2s, at instant speed after the first spell.
Twinflame :: the sorcery Dualcaster copies. Two mana.
Heat Shimmer :: the second copy of that half.
Either loop is lethal on its own with haste, and instantly lethal with any engine out: Impact Tremors is 3 per creature to every opponent, and Goblin Bombardment turns each one into 3 more.
Heartless Hidetsugu with Torbran and one doubler — Twinflame Tyrant, Solphim or Fiendish Duo — kills every opponent at once. Half their life doubled is at least their life minus one, and Torbran's 2 finishes it. You only lose half of yours. Hidetsugu has to tap, so give it haste with Lightning Greaves or Hall of the Bandit Lord.
Razorkin Needlehead with Wheel of Fortune makes every opponent draw seven — 21 damage each with Torbran out, 28 with Mechanized Warfare too.

# Surviving your own deck
The One Ring :: cast it and you have protection from everything until your next turn — so your own Earthquake, Flame Rift and Themberchaud cannot hurt you that turn. Plan your biggest turn around it. The upkeep life loss from its burden counters is the price.
Brash Taunter :: indestructible, and every point of damage it takes hits an opponent too, plus 2. Your own sweepers become extra burn instead of a cost.
Your real protection is the maths. You take each hit once; three opponents take it three times, plus 2 each. They run out first.

# Mana
Sol Ring :: turn one acceleration toward a turn-three Torbran.
Arcane Signet :: fixing that is just a mana rock in mono-red.
Fellwar Stone :: usually taps for red.
Mind Stone :: ramp early, a card later.
Ruby Medallion :: every red spell costs one less — nearly the whole deck.
Seething Song :: five red for three, which is a huge Earthquake.
Jeska's Will :: with your commander out, both halves — red mana per card in an opponent's hand and three cards to play.
Birgi, God of Storytelling :: a red mana for every spell you cast, and it does not empty between phases.
Neheb, the Eternal :: in your postcombat main phase, a red mana for every point of life your opponents lost this turn. After a Flame Rift that is eighteen mana for Pyrohemia.
Nykthos, Shrine to Nyx :: mono-red devotion adds up fast — Torbran alone is three.
Valakut, the Molten Pinnacle :: 3 damage anywhere for every Mountain you play once you control five others. Remember Torbran does not boost it.
Castle Embereth :: +1/+0 to the team for the combo swing.
Hall of the Bandit Lord :: haste for Heartless Hidetsugu or Kiki-Jiki the turn they land.
Sokenzan, Crucible of Defiance :: channel it for two hasty 1/1s — two Impact Tremors triggers.
War Room :: a card for one life and three mana, every turn.

# Finding the pieces
Gamble :: any card. The random discard hurts less in a deck this redundant.
Imperial Recruiter :: any creature with power 2 or less — Kiki-Jiki, Dualcaster Mage, Brash Taunter, Ashling or Razorkin Needlehead.
Goblin Matron :: any Goblin — Kiki-Jiki, Brash Taunter or Goblin Chainwhirler.

# Cards
Wheel of Fortune :: a fresh seven for everyone. With Razorkin Needlehead it is a finisher.
Wheel of Misfortune :: the same refill, with a damage mind-game attached.
Chandra, Torch of Defiance :: an impulse card or 2 damage to each opponent every turn — 4 with Torbran.
Light Up the Stage :: two cards for {R} once an opponent has lost life this turn, which is almost always.
Faithless Looting :: filtering, twice.
Valakut Awakening :: a land when you need one, or a hand refresh when you do not.

# Answers
Lightning Bolt :: 5 to a creature or a face with Torbran.
Abrade :: 3 damage or an artifact destroyed.
Fiery Confluence :: three modes, repeatable. Pick "2 damage to each opponent" three times for 12 to each with Torbran.
Blasphemous Act :: usually one mana, and it kills every creature — with Brash Taunter out, 15 of it goes at an opponent.
Chaos Warp :: the answer to an enchantment or planeswalker.
Vandalblast :: overloaded, a one-sided artifact wipe.
Pyroblast :: one mana against the blue deck holding a counterspell.

# What to keep in an opening hand
Keep a cheap engine and mana. Impact Tremors, Guttersnipe or Razorkin Needlehead on turn two, then Torbran on turn three or four, is the start that wins.
Deploy engines before sweepers. An Earthquake with nothing else out is fair; the same Earthquake after Impact Tremors, Guttersnipe and Torbran is a massacre.
Watch your own life. You take every symmetrical hit too. Cast The One Ring before your biggest turn, and do not let Descent into Avernus and Sulfuric Vortex tick you down while you have no clock on the table.
Hold a combo piece until you can win with it. Kiki-Jiki and Dualcaster Mage are just creatures until their partner lands — and they are the first thing an experienced table will kill.
